Synopsis
--------
The remote telnet server is affected by an authentication bypass vulnerability.

Description
-----------
The remote host is running GNU inetutils telnetd through version 2.7-2 which
is affected by an authentication bypass vulnerability (CVE-2026-24061).

By sending a crafted NEW_ENVIRON telnet option with the USER variable set to
"-f root", an unauthenticated remote attacker can bypass the authentication
mechanism and gain root-level shell access to the system. The "-f" flag is
interpreted by login(1) as a request to skip authentication for the specified
user, and the telnet daemon passes this environment variable to the login
process without proper sanitization.

This vulnerability is trivially exploitable and requires no authentication.
Public proof-of-concept exploit code is available.

CISA has added this vulnerability to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV)
catalog on 2026-04-15, indicating active exploitation in the wild.

Solution
--------
Upgrade GNU inetutils to version 2.8 or later.

As an immediate mitigation, consider disabling the telnet service entirely and
migrating to SSH for remote administration. If telnet cannot be disabled, apply
network-level access controls to restrict access to port 23/tcp to trusted
hosts only.

